# Changelog (engine/v1/)

## Texture color alpha fix, opacity removal, color/texture.color promotion

**What:** Fixed alpha being silently dropped during procedural texture generation — `RgbaToHex`/`parseHexColor` now round-trip through 8-digit hex instead of 6-digit. Deleted `generatedTexture.opacity`, deriving `mesh.material.transparent` from `primary.a`/`secondary.a` instead (`material.opacity` is now a constant `1`; alpha lives in the baked texture pixels). Promoted `color` (object-level) and `texture.color` as the modern, non-deprecated authoring surface; `primary`/`secondary` remain supported but are now `deprecated: true`. Replaced the undocumented pre-schema `hoistObjectColor` hack with real schema fields (`levelPart.color`, `levelObject.color`, `generatedTexture.color`) and a post-normalization fold.

**Why:** Alpha on `primary`/`secondary`/texture colors never worked — the hex round-trip had no room for it, and `generatedTexture.opacity` existed purely as a workaround scalar for that bug. The render shader already multiplies per-texel alpha into the tint uniform, so fixing generation was sufficient. The `color`/`primary`/`secondary` naming was also inconsistent: one authoring path (`hoistObjectColor`) was undocumented and bypassed schema validation entirely.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`RgbaToHex`/`parseHexColor`), [`engine/v1/core/canonSchemas.json`](engine/v1/core/canonSchemas.json) (`generatedTexture.opacity` removed; `generatedTexture.color`/`levelPart.color`/`levelObject.color` added; `secondary` marked deprecated), [`engine/v1/core/normalize.js`](engine/v1/core/normalize.js) (`hoistObjectColor` removed, `foldGeneratedColorAlias` added, `normalizePart`/`normalizeLevelObject` fold `color` into `primary`), [`engine/v1/builder/NewObject.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewObject.js) (`material.transparent`/`.opacity` derivation), [`engine/v1/builder/NewScatter.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewScatter.js) (instance tint alpha), plus content migration in `engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json`, `engine/v1/testGame/levels/entities.json`, `engine/v1/testGame/levels/levels.json`.

## Texture color alpha fix — dedup follow-up

**What:** Resolved the two duplication findings from the same-day audit of the change above. `normalize.js`'s `foldGeneratedColorAlias` was generalized into `foldColorAlias(source, sourceKey, dest, destKey)`, used at all four fold call sites (`normalizeTexture`, `normalizeOverrideTexture`, `normalizePart`, `normalizeLevelObject`); this also standardized fold behavior to always delete the source key, removing a null-handling inconsistency between the texture-layer and part/object-layer folds. The duplicated transparency predicate in `NewObject.js`'s two mesh-construction branches was extracted into a new exported `IsTextureTransparent(generatedTexture)` in `NewTexture.js`. Also stripped 45 now-schema-dead `"opacity": 1` keys left over in content JSON after the prior pass deleted `generatedTexture.opacity`.

**Why:** Author-approved cleanup of duplication flagged during review, on the grounds that a transparency check is likely reusable elsewhere and the fold pattern already had one prior extraction (`foldGeneratedColorAlias`) to be consistent with.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/core/normalize.js`](engine/v1/core/normalize.js) (`foldColorAlias`), [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`IsTextureTransparent`, exported), [`engine/v1/builder/NewObject.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewObject.js) (both call sites), `engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json`, `engine/v1/testGame/levels/entities.json`, `engine/v1/testGame/levels/levels.json` (dead-key removal).

## Texture color alpha fix — water transparency regression fix

**What:** Water rendered fully opaque instead of translucent, a regression introduced by the two changes above. Fixed by setting `primary.a`/`secondary.a` directly to the former `opacity` value (`0.5`) on the water body and water top texture blocks in `buildWaterVisualMeshes`, and removing the now-dead `opacity` keys there and in `buildTriggerMesh`'s trigger-visualization texture block (harmless there — `primary` was already the same object reference as the trigger's `color`, so alpha already flowed correctly — but the key was dead and removed for cleanliness).

**Why:** `engine/v1/builder/NewLevel.js` constructs its `texture.generated` object literals directly and passes them straight into `BuildObject`, bypassing `core/normalize.js` entirely — so this direct-construction path was never touched by the fold/deprecation machinery from the original pass and still carried the old `opacity` key, silently dead once `NewObject.js` stopped reading it. Two of the three blocks hardcoded `primary.a`/`secondary.a` to `1` while relying on the dead `opacity: 0.5` for the actual translucency. Caught by the user's own visual testing, not ARGUS.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/NewLevel.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewLevel.js) (`buildWaterVisualMeshes`, `buildTriggerMesh`).

## Texture color alpha fix — primary/secondary double-compositing bug

**What:** `drawPattern` and `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas` in `engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js` fill the canvas with `primary`, then draw `secondary` specks/blocks on top using the canvas default `globalCompositeOperation` (`source-over`). With real per-pixel alpha now baked into the canvas, `source-over` blends `secondary` over the already-semi-transparent `primary` pixel beneath it instead of replacing it — every speck/tile/stripe/grid region ends up as a mixed primary+secondary color at a *compounded* alpha (two colors both at `0.5` alpha compound to `0.75` via Porter-Duff over), rather than pure `secondary` at its own authored alpha. This read visually as a muddied/greenish hue and general oversaturation in patterned regions (most visible on water). Fixed by setting `ctx.globalCompositeOperation = "copy"` at the start of both functions, so every draw replaces the destination pixel outright — each pixel ends up as exactly one authored color at exactly that color's own alpha.

**Why:** This bug was invisible before the alpha fix — when `primary`/`secondary` alpha was always forced to `1`, `source-over` and `copy` behave identically, so `secondary` always cleanly overwrote `primary` regardless. It only became visible once alpha started carrying real values. Root-caused directly from a user visual report (screenshots of before/after) after an initial incorrect hypothesis (background/fog color bleeding through) was ruled out by the user.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`drawPattern`, `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas`). Not yet visually re-verified by the user — pending.

## Texture color alpha fix — correction to the double-compositing fix above

**What:** The `"copy"` fix in the entry above was itself wrong and caused a severe regression (level rendering broken across the board — reported by the user as magenta shapes and checkerboard chaos). `globalCompositeOperation = "copy"` does not mean "replace only within the newly drawn shape" — it replaces the *entire* canvas with just that draw call's shape, clearing everything outside it (including all earlier draws) to transparent. Since `tiles`/`stripes`/`noise`/`grid` draw `secondary` one shape at a time in a loop (up to 16000 times for `noise`), every subsequent draw wiped out everything before it, including the `primary` base fill. Replaced with a `fillReplacing(ctx, drawFn)` helper: runs `drawFn` once under `destination-out` with an opaque mask (punches a hole matching the shape's exact footprint, safe to repeat since it only affects pixels the shape covers) and once under `source-over` with the real fill style (paints the real color into that now-clean hole). `drawShape` now wraps its whole body in `fillReplacing` (covers the `noise` pattern in both functions, since they share it); the raw `fillRect` calls in `tiles`/`stripes`/`grid` are each wrapped individually. `radial` and the initial `primary` fill are untouched — both are single full-canvas draws, unaffected either way.

**Why:** Wrong assumption about Canvas 2D compositing semantics, asserted as a fix without verifying the spec. Doubles draw-call count in dense patterns, but this is one-time bake cost, not per-frame.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`fillReplacing`, `drawShape`, `drawPattern`, `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas`). Not yet visually re-verified by the user — pending, and given the previous attempt looked correct but wasn't, this should not be treated as confirmed until it actually renders correctly.

## Texture presets converted to rgba; water authored as real presets

**What:** The `textures` block of the texture preset library now authors `primary`/`secondary` as `{r,g,b,a}` objects instead of 6-digit hex strings, matching the convention the rest of the engine (and the same file's `scatterTypes` block) already used. `parseHexColor` and `RgbaToHex` are deleted; a module-scope `rgbaToCss` feeds the two canvas-paint sites, and `formatColorKey` — extracted from the `fmt` arrow formerly inside `ComputeGeneratedTextureID` — is now shared with `buildFaceTextureSignature`. `shape` became a valid preset field, honored on both the registry path and the per-face path. The zero-consumer `water-surface` preset was repurposed as `underwater` (water body); `sea-surface` was retargeted exclusively at the water top; `buildWaterVisualMeshes` now references both by id with neutral overrides instead of carrying inline color and pattern literals. The water top was retuned: `density` 1 → 0.35 (foam coverage 63.2% → 29.5%), base blue darkened, foam set near-white.

**Why:** The hex round-trip converted per-instance rgba overrides *to* hex and back to a CSS `rgba()` string, quantizing every channel to 8 bits for no reason — it was the original cause of the alpha loss fixed earlier. Authoring the water in `NewLevel.js` as JS literals meant two meshes shared one preset while overriding nearly all of it, leaving the preset's own colors unreachable. The "too much white" symptom was a misdiagnosis of `speckSize`: noise coverage is `1 - exp(-density × shapeFactor)`, in which `speckSize` cancels out entirely and only sets grain size, so `density` is the sole control over the blue/foam split.

**Note — one silent bug averted:** `buildFaceTextureSignature` built its `colorKey` by interpolating the two blueprint colors directly into a template literal. Once those became objects both would have stringified to `[object Object]`, collapsing the key so that any two noise faces differing only by color would share one baked canvas through the `getOrBuildFaceTexture` memo gate. Routing both through `formatColorKey` closes it.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json`](engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json) (all 11 preset entries), [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`rgbaToCss`, `formatColorKey`, `ComputeGeneratedTextureID`, `drawPattern`, `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas`, `createTextureRegistry`, `ResolveNoiseFaceBlueprint`, `compositeShapeDecal`, `buildFaceTextureSignature`), [`engine/v1/builder/NewLevel.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewLevel.js) (`buildWaterVisualMeshes`). Colour values are starting points for the author's visual tuning, not final.

## `sea-surface` figure/ground inversion

**What:** Swapped `primary` and `secondary` on the `sea-surface` preset, so the base fill is now the near-white foam and the specks are the water blue; that blue was also deepened to `{ 0.09, 0.28, 0.5 }`. Data-only — no code changed, and the per-instance `density` override in `buildWaterVisualMeshes` was deliberately left alone (the author is tuning it live).

**Why:** The water top read as sky-with-clouds rather than ocean. At roughly even coverage the color drawn as **discrete specks** reads as figure and the base fill reads as ground, so white blobs over blue read as objects sitting on a surface. Inverted, the white becomes the connected *interstitial* network between blue patches — which is how real ocean foam behaves, foam being the gaps between water rather than objects on top of it. The blue was additionally too pale to read as ocean depth.

**Consequence for authoring:** `density` on this preset has inverted meaning. Coverage is still `1 - exp(-density × shapeFactor)` and still describes the **secondary** color, but secondary is now the blue — so raising `density` now increases *blue* coverage, where previously it increased foam.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json`](engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json) (`sea-surface`). This is the one preset where `primary` is deliberately not the dominant/"own" color of the surface — do not "correct" it back.

## `fillReplacing` erase pass restricted to translucent paint

**What:** `fillReplacing` gained a `replace` parameter and short-circuits to a single plain draw when it is false. The flag is derived once per bake as `secondary.a < 1`, in both `drawPattern` and `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas`, and threaded through `drawShape` (new parameter) and the three raw `fillRect` sites in the `tiles`/`stripes`/`grid` branches. `radial` is untouched — it never used the helper.

**Why:** The erase-then-paint pair was damaging antialiased speck rims on fully-opaque textures. At an edge pixel with coverage `c` over an opaque base it yields `c + (1-c)²` alpha — `0.75` at `c = 0.5` — where plain `source-over` yields a correct `1`. This put pinholes in the speck edges of all 9 opaque presets (visible on `grass-soft`). The erase pass exists only to stop a *translucent* secondary compounding with the primary beneath it; against opaque paint `source-over` already covers the base pixel completely, so the erase achieves nothing and only eats the rim. The two water presets (`underwater`, `sea-surface`) still take the erase path, preserving the deliberate edge artifact that supplies the water body's entire visible variation.

**Settled semantics:** a translucent `secondary` over an opaque `primary` means "this region is partly transparent and shows the scene behind it", not "this region blends its color into the primary". That is the correct reading now that texture alpha is baked per-texel and composited against the scene, and it is what the water depends on.

**Where:** [`engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`fillReplacing`, `drawShape`, `drawPattern`, `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas`).

## Author-controlled `compositeMode`; texture-id collision fix

**What:** New `generatedTexture.compositeMode` field (`"replace"` / `"overlay"` / `null`), authorable both as a preset field and as a per-instance override. `"replace"` erases before painting so a translucent element fully replaces the base pixel; `"overlay"` paints plainly so it blends and compounds with what is beneath; `null` derives from `secondary.a < 1`, the behaviour that predates the field. A module-scope `resolveReplace` now supplies the boolean that `drawPattern` and `BuildNoiseFaceCanvas` previously each derived inline. The field is threaded through `createUsageEntry`, `registerTextureUsage`, `textureRegistrationOptions`, `createTextureRegistry`'s spread, and `ResolveNoiseFaceBlueprint`, and enters `buildFaceTextureSignature`'s `colorKey`.

`ComputeGeneratedTextureID` was rewritten in the same pass: it now appends a segment for every field that changes the baked canvas (`p=`, `s=`, `sh=`, `cm=`, `d=`, `ss=`) and its early return is gone.

**Why:** Deriving the composite behaviour from alpha is a sound default but made deliberate blending of two translucent colors unreachable. Separately, `ComputeGeneratedTextureID` previously returned the bare preset id unless a *color* override existed, so `shape`/`density`/`speckSize` overrides never reached the texture id — and because `registerTextureUsage` is last-writer-wins on a shared registry entry, two meshes on one preset differing only in those rendered with whichever registered last. Dropping the early return costs nothing: `join("::")` on a single-element array already yields the bare id, so neutral content keeps its original key and only previously-colliding content gains distinct entries.

**Note — no fallback anywhere:** engine-owned template JSON bypasses schema fallbacks, so the key would have been *absent* there while payload data has it as `null`. Absorbing that with `?? null` is forbidden (`FORBIDDEN_DEFENSIVE_CHECKS.md` §1, §3B, §5A). Instead all 92 authoring sites were populated explicitly — 78 `texture.generated` data literals, the 3 synthesized in `NewLevel.js`, and the 11 presets — so the field is genuinely guaranteed and read directly.

**Also:** the 2026-08-18 `opacity` removal had missed engine template JSON entirely; 67 dead `texture.generated.opacity` keys were stripped here. `texture.custom[].opacity` on image decals is live (handled off-schema in `normalize.js`) and all 29 were preserved.

**Where:** [`canonSchemas.json`](engine/v1/core/canonSchemas.json) (`generatedTexture.compositeMode`), [`NewTexture.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewTexture.js) (`resolveReplace`, `ComputeGeneratedTextureID`, `buildFaceTextureSignature`, the usage/registry/face-blueprint chain), [`NewLevel.js`](engine/v1/builder/NewLevel.js), [`templates/textures.json`](engine/v1/builder/templates/textures.json), plus `player/characters.json`, `templates/characters.json`, `templates/particles.json`, `templates/levels.json`. `templates/terrain.json` and `obstacles.json` are `{}` today, so whoever first authors a texture there must include the key.
